# Allowed signers for commit signatures: the trust root of the signature check
# in scripts/check-commit-hygiene.py, which Principle I of
# .specify/memory/constitution.md requires. The format is OpenSSH
# allowed_signers, one key per line, the principal first:
#
#   capintobe@gmail.com ssh-ed25519 AAAA...
#
# The principal MUST be the founder's address, exactly as above; the check
# rejects a signature made under any other principal. Do not paste a .pub line
# unchanged: it begins with the key type, so it has no principal, and the check
# refuses the file rather than failing every commit with a misleading reason.
# Keep the file free of blank lines; ssh-keygen warns on each one.
#
# THE FOUNDER'S PUBLIC KEY GOES HERE. Until a key is listed, the check reports
# that signing is not yet enabled and skips, so nothing is enforced yet. Only
# the founder can change that, because only the founder holds the key.
#
# What is missing is the key, not the signing. Commits here already carry SSH
# signatures; without a key to check them against, a signature is a claim
# nobody can verify. Listing the public key below is what turns it into one
# this repository can check. Add it in a signed commit and keep signing. On the
# founder's machine, once per clone:
#
#   git config gpg.format ssh
#   git config user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
#   git config commit.gpgsign true
#   git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile \
#     "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.github/allowed-signers"
#
# CI verifies against the copy of this file on the base branch, never the copy
# in the pull request, so a pull request cannot authorise its own key. A key
# therefore takes effect one merge after it lands, and the pull request that
# adds the first key passes as not yet enabled. What enforcement needs beyond
# this file is recorded in docs/governance/branch-protection.md.
